<h2>What a status report is, and who files it</h2>
<p>The expression <em>status report</em> does not appear as a defined term in the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023. It is a creature of practice: a report prepared by the investigating officer, routed through the station house officer and the office of the public prosecutor, and placed before the court that is seized of a bail or anticipatory bail application. In the Sessions Courts and the High Court of Delhi, it is tendered by the Additional Public Prosecutor appearing for the State, who also makes oral submissions on its basis. The report speaks for the investigating agency as of a particular date; it is not a chargesheet, not a final report, and not evidence.</p>
<p>Courts call for it because an application under Section 482 BNSS or a regular bail application must be decided on more than the FIR and the applicant’s own affidavit. The report tells the judge where the investigation actually stands on the day of hearing, which is often decisive on questions such as the need for custodial interrogation or the risk of evidence being tampered with.</p>

<h2>What a status report typically contains</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li><strong>Stage of investigation</strong> — whether statements have been recorded, whether the applicant has been examined or has joined the investigation, and what steps remain.</li>
<li><strong>Role attributed</strong> — the specific part the applicant is alleged to have played, as distinct from the omnibus allegations in the FIR.</li>
<li><strong>Recoveries</strong> — articles, documents, devices or money recovered, and whether any recovery is still sought from or at the instance of the applicant.</li>
<li><strong>Antecedents</strong> — previous involvements of the applicant, if any, as reflected in police records.</li>
<li><strong>Objection to relief</strong> — the agency’s stand on whether protection should be granted, and the reasons: apprehension of flight, influence over witnesses, or the need for custodial interrogation.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>A careful reader notices what the report does <em>not</em> say as much as what it says. A report that records cooperation, discloses no recovery pending from the applicant, and mentions no antecedents has, in substance, conceded much of the ground on which protection is ordinarily opposed.</p>

<h2>Does the applicant see it?</h2>
<p>There is no statutory right to the status report, but the prevailing practice in Delhi is disclosure: an advance copy is supplied to counsel for the applicant, or a copy is handed across the bar before arguments begin, so that the hearing is not conducted on material the applicant has never seen. Where the agency asserts that disclosure would prejudice the investigation, portions may be placed in a sealed cover, and the court decides how far it will rely on undisclosed material while deciding a question of personal liberty. Counsel should always ask, on the record, for a copy of the report and note whether it was supplied.</p>

<h2>The case diary: a different regime altogether</h2>
<p>The case diary stands on an entirely different footing. Under Section 192 BNSS (formerly Section 172 CrPC), every investigating officer must maintain a diary recording, day by day, the time at which information reached him, the times of beginning and closing the investigation, the places visited, and a statement of the circumstances ascertained. The court may send for the diaries and may use them — not as evidence in the case, but to <em>aid</em> it in the inquiry or trial, and the same aid is routinely taken at the bail stage.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h4>Status report</h4><p>A practice document prepared for the hearing, tendered through the prosecutor, ordinarily disclosed to the applicant, and open to rejoinder and argument.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h4>Case diary</h4><p>A statutory record under Section 192 BNSS, perused by the judge alone; the accused has no right to call for it or see it merely because the court has referred to it.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The statute carves out one narrow window. If the police officer who made the diary uses it to refresh his memory, or if the court uses it for the purpose of contradicting that officer, the provisions of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 governing refreshing memory and contradiction apply, and to that limited extent the defence gains access. Outside that window, an application demanding the diary at the bail stage is misconceived and is routinely declined.</p>

<h2>Responding to an adverse status report</h2>
<p>Because the report is a version and not evidence, the proper response is measured and documentary. Counsel may seek leave to file a short rejoinder confined to verifiable facts: dated acknowledgments of appearance before the investigating officer, written replies to notices, proof that devices or documents sought have been handed over, travel and medical records that answer an allegation of evasion. Inconsistencies within the report — a claim that the applicant is absconding beside an admission that he appeared on summons — should be flagged precisely, with paragraph references. What rarely helps is a rejoinder that re-argues the merits of the FIR; the bail court is not conducting a mini-trial, and prolixity dilutes the few points that matter.</p>

<h2>Practice in Delhi courts</h2>
<p>Two features of Delhi practice deserve mention. First, advance service: applications are ordinarily served in advance on the State through the prosecutor’s office, and courts expect the status report to be ready on the returnable date. Second, adjournments: where the report is not filed, the court usually grants a short accommodation to the State, and where the applicant faces imminent arrest it will often continue or grant interim protection so that the delay does not itself decide the application. Counsel for the applicant should diarise the returnable date, confirm beforehand with the prosecutor’s branch whether the report has been received, and be ready to argue on the available record if the court declines a further adjournment.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The status report is argued; the case diary is perused. Keeping the two regimes distinct — pressing for disclosure and rejoinder on the first, and not demanding what the statute withholds on the second — marks out a hearing that assists the court, and a record of documented cooperation remains the most effective answer to an adverse report.</p>
</div>
